---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Container from "../components/ui/Container.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../utils/constants";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Contact"
  description="How to reach Open Waters: email, GitHub issues, or Discord, depending on what you need."
>
  <Container class="py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="mb-8">Contact</h1>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
        <p class="text-fg-muted text-xl leading-relaxed">
          Open Waters is run by Open Water Software, LLC, a small operation
          rather than a support department. Mail gets read and answered; picking
          the right place below just gets you an answer faster.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Email</h2>

        <p>
          <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> reaches us for anything:
          licensing questions, a higher rate limit for a production application, a
          commercial AIS tier, data or partnership enquiries, press, or anything about
          the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Bugs and feature requests</h2>

        <p>
          These belong in the issue tracker of the project they concern, on <a
            href="https://github.com/openwatersio">GitHub</a
          >, where they are public and get fixed in the open. A wrong tide
          prediction goes to the tide engine or the station database, a chart
          rendering problem to the chart style, and a stream that drops out to
          the AIS server. If you are not sure which, open it anywhere or email
          us and we will move it.
        </p>

        <p>
          A wrong prediction is much easier to fix with the station id, the time
          you asked about, and what you expected instead.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Questions and conversation</h2>

        <p>
          <a href="https://discord.gg/NeMyrYMGr">Discord</a> is the place for questions
          that are not yet bugs, for showing what you built, and for talking through
          an approach before you write it. Contributing a receiver to the AIS network,
          or depth soundings from your own instruments, usually starts as a conversation
          there.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Not for navigation</h2>

        <p>
          Nothing published here is fit for navigation, and no answer over email
          changes that. Official charts and tide tables from your hydrographic
          office remain the reference on the water. If you have found a
          discrepancy between an Open Waters prediction and an official one,
          that is worth reporting, and worth trusting the official one in the
          meantime.
        </p>

        <p class="text-fg-faint text-sm">
          Open Water Software, LLC · <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
            >{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</MainLayout>
